/* ==============================================================
   cold_start — guarantee the robot behaves the way you expect
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   PURPOSE   Wipe every leftover setting, so a demo behaves the
             same on a robot someone else has been fiddling with
   SAVE AS   00_common/00_cold_start
   CALL      StartProgram("00_common/00_cold_start")
   NEEDS     Robot powered on, motors powered (RESET pressed),
             connected in CONTROL mode
   MOVES     Nothing. See the note on homing below.
   TIME      ~10 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE PROBLEM

   The robot on the bench was last used by someone else.
   Did they leave a tool frame defined? A restricted joint limit?
   A work zone? The time-scaling slider at 20%? Simulation mode
   on?

   Any one of those makes your demo behave differently from the
   way it behaved when you rehearsed it, and you will not know
   why without checking.

   THE THREE KINDS OF SETTING

   This is the distinction that makes the whole thing tractable,
   and it is worth knowing cold. The MCS500 has three kinds, not
   two.

   1. NON-PERSISTENT settings live only for as long as the robot
      is activated. TRF, WRF, speeds, blending, payload, posture
      and turn selection, torque limits, move mode, MoveJump
      heights. The manual puts it plainly: by deactivating the
      robot, you lose all settings that are not persistent.

      So DEACTIVATE + REACTIVATE resets all of them, in one
      stroke, back to firmware defaults. That is Part 3 below.

   2. REBOOT-ONLY settings survive a deactivation and are only
      reset when the robot is power cycled. Time scaling and
      recovery mode are the two that matter for motion.
      Deactivating does nothing to them, so a robot
      that has been on all day can still be running at the speed
      someone set on the slider this morning. That is Part 2.

   3. PERSISTENT settings are written to the robot's SD card and
      survive deactivation, reboots, and power cycles. Only 14
      commands set these — the manual calls them configuration
      commands — and deactivating does NOTHING to them.

      These are the ones that will surprise you, because there is
      no natural moment at which they get cleared. They have to
      be dealt with deliberately. That is Parts 1 and 4.

   THE HAPPY ACCIDENT

   Every persistent setting that affects MOTION can only be
   changed while the robot is DEACTIVATED. Which means the
   deactivate step you need anyway is exactly the window in which
   you can fix them. That is why this program is shaped the way
   it is.

   (The rest of the fourteen are network and identity settings —
   the robot name, its IP options, and which fieldbus it speaks.
   None affect motion, and this program leaves all of them
   alone.)

   DOES THIS MOVE THE ROBOT?

   No. The MCS500 has absolute encoders and never needs homing,
   so deactivating and reactivating it does not move the arm.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============================================================
   PART 1 — AUDIT: see what you are dealing with
   ============================================================

   Run this part FIRST, on its own, and read the log panel.
   Nothing here changes anything — these are all data requests.

   This is the safe answer to "is this robot set up the way I
   expect?", and on a customer's own robot it is often the only
   part of this file you should run. Look before you reset. */

GetFwVersion()              // are the examples written for this firmware?
GetProductType()            // MUST answer [2084][MCS500] — see below
GetRobotSerial()

GetJointLimitsCfg()         // [2094][1] means USER JOINT LIMITS ARE ACTIVE
GetWorkZoneLimits()         // a cuboid tighter than +/-10000 is configured
GetWorkZoneCfg()            // severity and what gets checked
GetToolSphere()             // non-zero radius means a tool is declared
GetCollisionCfg()
GetPStop2Cfg()              // what the P-Stop 2 safety input does
GetCalibrationCfg()
GetSimModeCfg()             // which sim mode ActivateSim() would pick

GetTimeScaling()            // anything under 100 slows EVERYTHING
GetRecoveryMode()           // on = reduced speed, relaxed limits

GetStatusRobot()            // activated? in error? in sim mode?

/* Reading the answers:

     GetJointLimitsCfg  -> 0  factory limits                  GOOD
                           1  someone restricted a joint
     GetWorkZoneLimits  -> +/-10000 on every axis             GOOD
                           anything tighter is a configured cage
     GetWorkZoneCfg     -> 4, 1  factory default              GOOD
     GetToolSphere      -> 0, 0, 0, 0  no tool declared       GOOD
     GetCollisionCfg    -> 4  factory default                 GOOD
     GetPStop2Cfg       -> 3  factory default (clear motion)  GOOD
     GetCalibrationCfg  -> 1  factory default                 GOOD
     GetSimModeCfg      -> 1  normal (real-time) simulation   GOOD
     GetTimeScaling     -> 100                                GOOD
     GetRecoveryMode    -> 0  off                             GOOD

   If all of those read as GOOD, you do not need Part 4 at all —
   Parts 2 and 3 alone will give you a clean robot.

   /!\ CHECK GetProductType FIRST. This repo is written for the
     MCS500 and nothing else. Every example assumes the four-axis
     SCARA geometry — four-value joint sets with a LINEAR third
     joint in millimetres, four-value poses, the MCS500's joint
     limits, and one posture parameter. On any other Mecademic
     model those assumptions are wrong, and the programs will
     either be refused or move somewhere you did not intend. If
     this is not an MCS500, stop here. */


/* ============================================================
   PART 2 — THE SETTINGS ONLY A REBOOT CLEARS
   ============================================================

   Both of these run in any robot state, and both are safe.

   Time scaling is the slider at the bottom of the MecaPortal's
   program panel. Below 100 it slows every motion AND every
   Delay by the same factor — the path is identical, everything
   just takes longer. Left at 30% by someone else, it makes your
   demo look like a robot three times slower than it is. */
SetTimeScaling(100)

/* Recovery mode exists to get a robot out of trouble — outside
   a joint limit, outside the work zone, in self-collision. It
   does that by relaxing those checks and capping speed hard.
   Nobody should be running a demo in it.

   If this robot actually NEEDS recovery mode — a joint parked
   outside its user limits — then the ActivateRobot in Part 5
   will fail once this line has run. That is the right outcome:
   you have just found the problem. Jog it back in recovery mode
   from the MecaPortal, then run this file again. */
SetRecoveryMode(0)


/* ============================================================
   PART 3 — THE RESET THAT IS ALWAYS SAFE
   ============================================================

   Deactivate, then reactivate. Everything non-persistent goes
   back to its firmware default: TRF and WRF back to the flange
   and the base, speeds back to 25% and 150 mm/s, blending back
   to 100%, payload back to 0, torque limits back to 100%,
   posture and turn selection back to automatic, move mode back
   to velocity-based, MoveJump heights back to 10 mm.

   No editing required, nothing cell-specific, nothing
   destructive. If you only run one part of this file, run this
   one. */

DeactivateRobot()

/* --- the robot is now deactivated -----------------------------
   This is the ONLY window in which Part 4 can run. If you want
   it, uncomment it now — it has to sit here, between the
   deactivate and the activate. */


/* ============================================================
   PART 4 — PERSISTENT SETTINGS  (opt-in; read the warning)
   ============================================================

   /!\ WARNING — DO NOT UNCOMMENT THIS ON A CUSTOMER'S PRODUCTION
     ROBOT WITHOUT ASKING.

     These lines return the persistent settings to their FACTORY
     defaults. On a bare demo robot that is exactly what you
     want. On a robot in a real cell, several of them are the
     safety envelope somebody configured deliberately — a work
     zone that stops the arm reaching the loading window, a joint
     limit protecting a tool cable, a P-Stop 2 behaviour chosen
     in a risk assessment. Resetting those silently removes
     protection that the cell was built around.

     Rule of thumb: uncomment on a demo or loaner robot. On a
     customer's own robot, run Part 1, show them what it says,
     and let them decide.

   These are commented out deliberately. Uncomment as a block. */

// SetJointLimitsCfg(0)                    // factory joint limits
// SetWorkZoneLimits(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)     // the zeros form resets to factory
// SetWorkZoneCfg(4, 1)                    // factory: error on breach, check FCP
// SetToolSphere(0, 0, 0, 0)               // no tool declared
// SetCollisionCfg(4)                      // factory: warn and raise a motion error
// SetPStop2Cfg(3)                         // factory: P-Stop 2 clears the motion queue
// SetCalibrationCfg(1)                    // factory: calibration ENABLED
// SetSimModeCfg(1)                        // factory: normal real-time sim

/* Notes on four of those:

   SetJointLimitsCfg(0) DISABLES user-defined limits and restores
   the factory ones. It does not erase the user limits — they
   stay in memory and can be re-enabled later with
   SetJointLimitsCfg(1). Nothing is lost.

   SetCalibrationCfg(1) matters more than it looks. If the robot
   went through Mecademic's optional calibration service, turning
   calibration off sends Cartesian moves to slightly different
   poses — MoveJoints is unaffected, MovePose and MoveLin are
   not. A robot with calibration switched off is a robot whose
   accuracy quietly does not match what was paid for.

   SetWorkZoneLimits(0,0,0,0,0,0) is the documented reset form.
   All-zeros means "factory default", which is +/-10,000 mm on
   every axis — effectively no work zone at all.

   SetPStop2Cfg decides what the robot does when the P-Stop 2
   safety input opens: 3 pauses and CLEARS the motion queue
   (factory), 2 pauses and keeps it, so a ResumeMotion carries on
   where it stopped. That is a safety-design decision. If Part 1
   read 2, somebody chose it — find out why before changing it.

   NOT TOUCHED HERE, ON PURPOSE:

     SetRobotName, SetNetworkOptions, EnableEtherNetIp,
     EnableProfinet, SwitchToEtherCat

   Those are persistent too, but they are the robot's identity
   and how it is wired into the customer's network. Resetting
   them from a demo script would be rude at best and would take
   the robot off the network at worst.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============================================================
   PART 5 — BACK TO READY FOR MOTION
   ============================================================

   The same three commands as basic/01, and for the same reasons.
   All idempotent, so none of them fails if there is nothing to
   do.

   There is no Home() here, and that is not an omission. The
   MCS500 knows where its joints are from the moment it powers
   up. (If you came from a Meca500 and add one anyway, the robot
   accepts it and does nothing — the manual says Home has effect
   only on the Meca500.) */

ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


/* ============================================================
   PART 6 — THE HOUSE SETTINGS
   ============================================================

   Part 3 left every non-persistent setting at its FIRMWARE
   default. That is a known state, but it is not necessarily
   OUR known state — this repo runs slower than the firmware
   default, and a real cell has a tool frame.

   So finish by applying the house settings. Save
   00_common/01_init.mxprog as "00_common/01_init" first, or
   comment this out. */

StartProgram("00_common/01_init")

LogTrace("00_common/00_cold_start complete")
SetCheckpoint(99)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   HOW TO USE THIS IN PRACTICE

   Before a demo, on a robot you do not know:
     1. Run Part 1 alone. Select those lines, press Ctrl+Enter,
        read the log.
     2. If anything reads as non-factory, decide whether it is
        deliberate. Ask, if it is not your robot.
     3. Run the whole file.

   On your own demo robot: just run the whole file, with Part 4
   uncommented. Ten seconds, and the robot is in a state you can
   predict.

   /!\ ONE THING TO VERIFY ON HARDWARE

   Deactivating from inside a running program is legal — the
   commands allow it, and every usage restriction here is
   satisfied in this order. But a deactivate clears the motion
   queue, and this repo was written without a robot to hand, so
   the MecaPortal's exact behaviour when its program runner meets
   a DeactivateRobot mid-file has not been confirmed.

   If the run stops at Part 3 rather than continuing, split this
   file in three and run them in order, or do the deactivate and
   reactivate with the MecaPortal toolbar buttons and run only
   Parts 2, 4 and 6. The command sequence is correct either way.
   Please delete this note once someone has run it on a robot.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
